# Example reverse proxy for Glosa's room stations (README's "Room stations
# (mini PC per stage)" section): getUserMedia only runs in a secure context,
# so a mini PC on the venue network needs Glosa served over real HTTPS, not
# plain http://<server>:8000.
#
# Copy this file, replace the placeholder below, and run Caddy next to (or
# in front of) Glosa -- as its own process, or as the commented-out `caddy`
# service in docker-compose.yml, which already mounts this file.
#
# --- Option A: a real domain (recommended for the venue) -------------------
# Caddy requests and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate on its own; the
# domain must resolve to this machine and ports 80/443 must be reachable
# from the internet for the ACME challenge.

station.example.com {
	reverse_proxy glosa:8000
}

# --- Option B: LAN only, no public domain -----------------------------------
# `tls internal` makes Caddy its own local certificate authority: every
# device that will open a station (or the admin panel) needs Caddy's root
# certificate installed once (`caddy trust`, or copy
# $XDG_DATA_HOME/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt to each mini PC and
# import it) -- after that, this hostname is a secure context for
# getUserMedia like any real HTTPS site. Comment out Option A above and
# uncomment this block instead; give the mini PCs' hosts file (or your DNS)
# an entry for the chosen hostname pointing at this machine.
#
# glosa.local {
# 	tls internal
# 	reverse_proxy glosa:8000
# }
